---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import providers from '../data/providers.json';
import prices from '../data/prices.json';
---
<Base title="About the data - CostOfCare">
  <h1 style="font-size:1.7rem;">Where the prices come from</h1>
  <p class="sub">Every price on this site is a real, published number - never an invented estimate. Here is exactly where each one comes from and how fresh it is.</p>

  <h2>The law that makes this possible</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>Since 2021, the <strong>CMS Hospital Price Transparency Rule</strong> requires every US hospital to publish its prices - cash prices and insurer-negotiated rates - in machine-readable files. The <strong>Transparency in Coverage</strong> rules require insurers to publish negotiated rates too. And since 2023, the <strong>No Surprises Act</strong> gives uninsured and self-pay patients the right to a written good-faith estimate before scheduled care.</p>
    <p class="muted" style="margin-top:8px">The catch: those files are enormous - insurer Transparency in Coverage feeds alone run to terabytes of raw JSON per month. So we take the practical route: hospital price-transparency files (the same prices, thousands of times smaller), providers' published self-pay rates, and independent services that parse the big insurer files, and we show you the source and date on every number. Direct insurer TiC feeds (each insurer publishes them - search "&lt;insurer name&gt; Transparency in Coverage files") are on the roadmap as we add cities.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Sources and dates</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <ul style="padding-left:18px; display:grid; gap:10px;">
      {providers.map((p) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{p.name}</strong> - <a href={p.source_url}>{p.source_label}</a>
          <div class="muted">Retrieved {prices.retrieved}. {p.locations}</div>
        </li>
      ))}
      <li>
        <strong>Austin-area benchmarks</strong> - <a href="https://www.carepriceguide.com/tx/austin/mri">CarePriceGuide</a> (city medians computed from 14 Austin-area hospitals' files) and <a href="https://medrates.fyi/hospital/st-davids-medical-center">MedRates</a> (per-plan negotiated-rate ranges).
        <div class="muted">Retrieved {prices.retrieved}.</div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>How to read these prices</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <ul style="padding-left:18px; display:grid; gap:6px;">
      <li><strong>Self-pay price</strong>: the provider's published cash price, or a published direct-pay program rate. Ranges mean the provider published more than one cash rate (for example by setting).</li>
      <li><strong>With insurance</strong>: negotiated rates from the provider's own file - "typical" is the rate most plans cluster around, "lowest" is the cheapest negotiated rate published. Your plan's rate may differ, and what <em>you</em> pay depends on your deductible and copay/coinsurance.</li>
      <li><strong>Hospital prices</strong> are often the facility fee only. Radiologists, anesthesiologists and pathologists may bill separately.</li>
      <li><strong>Prices go stale.</strong> Hospitals update these files on their own schedule (the law requires at least yearly; many update monthly). Each source above shows when we read it - confirm the current price with the provider before you book.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>What this site is not</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>This is price information, not medical advice, and not a quote. Coverage today: Austin, TX metro, common labs, imaging and ER facility fees. More cities and services are planned.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
